<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <ProjectPageHeader project-id="pwcRag" context="PwC" data-scroll-reveal>
      <h1>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>
      <p class="project-subtitle">Keeping rules and their conditions together when preparing PDFs for an AI assistant</p>
    </ProjectPageHeader>

    <article class="project-narrative" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        At PwC, I helped design how a public-enquiry system reads the rules published in complex PDFs. It used
        retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), giving a language model relevant passages to answer each question,
        so the quality of those passages mattered before the model began to write.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a rule is qualified by a note several pages later, reading the sentence alone can produce the wrong answer.
        The same problem appears when a table crosses a page break or a flowchart expresses a decision through its
        branches, because the relationship between the pieces is part of the meaning. Ordinary parsing and chunking
        could separate those pieces even though a person reading the PDF could follow them.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work on the knowledge-base ingestion pipeline focused on preserving those relationships before the
        documents were divided into searchable chunks. A retrieved rule would then arrive with the qualifications
        needed to interpret it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="project-chapter-first">An architecture shaped by the documents</h2>
      <p>
        MinerU, a structured PDF parser, handled text and table layout, leaving the relationships it missed for custom
        processing. For an explicit reference such as Note (4), I could locate the matching note with a rule-based
        linker and check that it had been reattached correctly. Flowcharts called for visual interpretation, so I
        used a vision-language model (VLM) to reconstruct their nodes, labels, and branches as Mermaid, a text-based
        diagram format. The resulting material could enter the same RAGFlow chunking and retrieval process.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sending every page through the VLM would have introduced costs and maintenance work the client would continue to
        bear after implementation. The architecture had to fit its existing infrastructure, and the team preferred
        to operate a single AI model where possible. I reserved the VLM for flowcharts, where the connections between
        shapes carried information that text extraction lost. Text, tables, and explicit references could be handled
        with more predictable methods whose output was easier to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        I tested these choices together in a proof of concept, then used the results, infrastructure requirements,
        and operating costs to develop the client recommendation. The pipeline below shows where each repair
        happens and how its effect carries through to the answer.
      </p>

